window.QUEST_CONTENT = {"game":{"title":"Quest for Automation","xp":{"firstTryBonus":0.5,"cleanBonus":0.25,"hintFloor":0.25},"levels":[{"xp":0,"title":"Intern"},{"xp":120,"title":"Junior Tester"},{"xp":280,"title":"Test Engineer"},{"xp":500,"title":"Automation Engineer"},{"xp":800,"title":"SDET"},{"xp":1200,"title":"Senior SDET"},{"xp":1700,"title":"Automation Lead"},{"xp":2300,"title":"Automation Archmage"}],"badges":[{"id":"first-blood","name":"First Green","icon":"✔","hint":"Clear your first challenge.","rule":{"type":"challengesPassed","value":1}},{"id":"never-slept","name":"Never Slept","icon":"⏱","hint":"Clear the Locator Forest without a single time.sleep().","rule":{"type":"zoneCleanOfSleep","zone":"locator-forest"}},{"id":"selector-sniper","name":"Selector Sniper","icon":"◎","hint":"Drive the page with 10 role-based locators.","rule":{"type":"statAtLeast","stat":"roleLocators","value":10}},{"id":"one-shot","name":"One Shot","icon":"⭐","hint":"Pass 5 challenges on the first try.","rule":{"type":"firstTryPasses","value":5}},{"id":"unaided","name":"Unaided","icon":"🧠","hint":"Pass 5 challenges without opening a hint.","rule":{"type":"passedWithoutHints","value":5}},{"id":"trailhead","name":"Trailhead","icon":"⛺","hint":"Clear Base Camp.","rule":{"type":"zoneCleared","zone":"base-camp"}},{"id":"pathfinder","name":"Pathfinder","icon":"🌲","hint":"Clear the Locator Forest.","rule":{"type":"zoneCleared","zone":"locator-forest"}},{"id":"web-first","name":"Web First","icon":"🌊","hint":"Clear Assertion Ridge.","rule":{"type":"zoneCleared","zone":"assertion-ridge"}},{"id":"boss-slayer","name":"Boss Slayer","icon":"👑","hint":"Beat your first boss challenge.","rule":{"type":"bossesCleared","value":1}},{"id":"triple-crown","name":"Triple Crown","icon":"🏆","hint":"Beat three boss challenges.","rule":{"type":"bossesCleared","value":3}}]},"zones":[{"id":"base-camp","order":0,"badge":"0","abbr":"BC","title":"Base Camp","tagline":"What a test actually is, and how to make one run.","teaches":"Arrange-Act-Assert, pytest naming, your first passing test","source":["guide §1 About Playwright","guide §5 Creating test scripts"],"requires":[],"map":{"x":8,"y":27},"objectives":["Explain what separates a test from a script that just clicks around","Name a test file and a test function so pytest actually collects them","Write the three beats of a test: Arrange, Act, Assert","Read a Playwright failure message and say what went wrong"],"plannedChallenges":[],"lesson":"<h3>A test is a script that can fail</h3><p>Automation means writing a program that uses a website the way a person would - clicking, typing, reading - and then <strong>checks</strong> that the site behaved. The check is the whole point. A script with no check can run green forever while the site is on fire.</p><p>That check is called an <strong>assertion</strong>, and in Playwright it looks like this:</p><pre class=\"lang-python\"><code>expect(page.get_by_text(&quot;Order placed!&quot;)).to_be_visible()</code></pre><h3>Two tools, two jobs</h3><ul><li><strong>Playwright</strong> drives the browser: <code>goto</code>, <code>click</code>, <code>fill</code>.</li><li><strong>pytest</strong> finds your tests, runs them, and tells you which failed.</li></ul><p>They meet in one line you will write at the top of every file:</p><pre class=\"lang-python\"><code>from playwright.sync_api import Page, expect</code></pre><p><code>page</code> is a fresh browser tab, handed to your test automatically. You never create it yourself.</p><h3>pytest only collects things named <code>test_</code></h3><p>This is a discovery rule, not a style preference:</p><ul><li>files must be named <code>test_*.py</code></li><li>functions inside them must be named <code>test_*</code></li></ul><p>Anything else is silently ignored - which is why &quot;my test does not run&quot; is almost always &quot;my test is not called <code>test_something</code>&quot;.</p><h3>Arrange, Act, Assert</h3><p>Nearly every good test has the same three beats:</p><ol><li><strong>Arrange</strong> - get ready: open the page, set up the data you need.</li><li><strong>Act</strong> - do the one thing you are testing.</li><li><strong>Assert</strong> - check the outcome.</li></ol><pre class=\"lang-python\"><code>def test_search_finds_dresses(page: Page):\n    page.goto(&quot;https://automationexercise.com/products&quot;)   # Arrange\n    page.locator(&quot;#search_product&quot;).fill(&quot;dress&quot;)          # Act\n    page.locator(&quot;#submit_search&quot;).click()                 # Act\n    expect(page.get_by_role(                               # Assert\n        &quot;heading&quot;, name=&quot;Searched Products&quot;)).to_be_visible()</code></pre><p>If your assert comes before your act, you are testing the page you started on. That mistake is Challenge 3.</p><div class=\"callout tip\"><strong>One behaviour per test</strong><p><code>test_login_with_wrong_password_shows_error</code> is one test. Do not also check the signup form in it. When it fails, its name should tell you what broke before you open the file.</p></div><h3>About the shop on the right</h3><p>It is a stand-in for <a href=\"https://automationexercise.com\">automationexercise.com</a> with the same ids, class names and headings. Anything you learn here points at the real thing.</p><p>One difference worth knowing: it has <strong>no real clock</strong>. Slow operations happen on a virtual timeline that only moves while <code>expect()</code> is waiting. That is not a cheat - it means &quot;the results take 600 ms to arrive&quot; is exactly reproducible, so auto-waiting can be taught honestly instead of hopefully.</p>","challenges":[{"id":"first-test","title":"Your first test","kind":"write-the-test","xp":30,"brief":"<p>The shop is open on the right. Finish this test so it checks that the browser tab&#x27;s title is <code>Automation Exercise</code>.</p><p>One line is all it takes. The matcher you want is <code>to_have_title</code>, and it goes on the <code>page</code>, not on a locator:</p><pre class=\"lang-python\"><code>expect(page).to_have_title(&quot;...&quot;)</code></pre>","scenario":{"route":"/"},"starter":"from playwright.sync_api import Page, expect\n\n\ndef test_home_page_has_the_right_title(page: Page):\n    # ARRANGE + ACT: open the shop's home page\n    page.goto(\"https://automationexercise.com/\")\n\n    # ASSERT: check the tab title. Your line goes here.\n","solution":"from playwright.sync_api import Page, expect\n\n\ndef test_home_page_has_the_right_title(page: Page):\n    page.goto(\"https://automationexercise.com/\")\n    expect(page).to_have_title(\"Automation Exercise\")\n","rubric":["test-function","has-assertion"],"checks":[{"kind":"matcher","name":"to_have_title","label":"You assert the page title with expect(page).to_have_title(...)","feedback":"I did not see a to_have_title check run. Note it goes on the page itself - expect(page), not expect(page.locator(...))."},{"kind":"state","path":"route","equals":"/","label":"The test is looking at the home page","feedback":"Your test navigated somewhere else. Leave the page.goto() line pointing at the home page for this one."}],"hints":[{"cost":3,"label":"Nudge","text":"expect() takes either a Locator or the page itself. Titles belong to the page."},{"cost":5,"label":"Strategy","text":"The shape is expect(page).to_have_title(\"...\"). The title is written in the browser tab above the shop panel - and it is the same one the kit's Test Case 1 checks."},{"cost":8,"label":"Solution","text":"expect(page).to_have_title(\"Automation Exercise\")"}],"reveal":"<p>That is a complete, honest automated test: it opens a page, it states a belief, and it can fail.</p><p>In the kit this exact line is the second line of <code>learn/test_tc01_register_user.py</code>. You have just written production code.</p>"},{"id":"what-pytest-collects","title":"What pytest actually collects","kind":"quiz","xp":20,"brief":"<p>&quot;It says 0 tests collected&quot; is the single most common first-day problem, and it is almost always a naming rule. Three questions.</p>","questions":[{"prompt":"<p>A file called <code>login_test.py</code> contains <code>def test_login(page): ...</code>. You run <code>pytest</code>. What happens?</p>","options":["It runs - the function is named test_login","It is ignored - the FILE must start with test_ too","It runs, but only with the --strict flag","It errors, because a test file cannot end in _test.py"],"answer":1,"why":"Both halves of the rule must hold: the file must match test_*.py AND the function must start with test_. Rename it to test_login.py and it is collected. (pytest can be configured to accept *_test.py as well, but the kit's pytest.ini does not - stick to the default and you will never be surprised.)"},{"prompt":"<p>Inside <code>test_cart.py</code> you write <code>def check_cart_total(page): ...</code>. Does pytest run it?</p>","options":["Yes - it is in a test file","No - the function name does not start with test_","Only if another test calls it","Only if it contains an assert"],"answer":1,"why":"pytest collects by NAME, not by content. check_cart_total is just a function sitting in a file that nobody calls. This is genuinely useful: helper functions live safely next to tests precisely because they are not named test_."},{"prompt":"<p>Where does the <code>page</code> argument come from in <code>def test_home(page):</code>?</p>","options":["You have to create it: page = Browser().new_page()","It is a pytest fixture from the pytest-playwright plugin - a fresh tab per test","It is a global variable pytest sets before the run","It is optional and always None unless you pass it yourself"],"answer":1,"why":"pytest matches the argument name to a registered fixture. pytest-playwright provides `page` and hands each test a brand-new browser tab, which is why tests cannot leak state into each other through the browser. The kit's conftest.py says exactly this in its opening comment."}],"reveal":"<p>Naming is discovery. If a test is not running, read its file name and its function name before you read anything else.</p>"},{"id":"arrange-act-assert","title":"Arrange, Act, Assert - in that order","kind":"fix-the-broken-test","xp":30,"brief":"<p>This test fails, and not because the shop is broken. Run it first and read the message - it tells you exactly which page it was looking at.</p><p>Then fix it. Do not add anything: just put the three beats in the right order.</p>","scenario":{"route":"/"},"starter":"from playwright.sync_api import Page, expect\n\n\ndef test_products_link_opens_the_products_page(page: Page):\n    # ARRANGE\n    page.goto(\"https://automationexercise.com/\")\n\n    # ASSERT\n    expect(page).to_have_url(\"https://automationexercise.com/products\")\n\n    # ACT\n    page.get_by_role(\"link\", name=\"Products\").click()\n","solution":"from playwright.sync_api import Page, expect\n\n\ndef test_products_link_opens_the_products_page(page: Page):\n    # ARRANGE\n    page.goto(\"https://automationexercise.com/\")\n\n    # ACT\n    page.get_by_role(\"link\", name=\"Products\").click()\n\n    # ASSERT\n    expect(page).to_have_url(\"https://automationexercise.com/products\")\n","rubric":["test-function","has-assertion"],"checks":[{"kind":"state","path":"route","equals":"/products","label":"The click actually happened and moved the shop to /products","feedback":"The shop is not on the products page at the end of your test. Did the click line run - or is it still sitting after the assertion?"},{"kind":"matcher","name":"to_have_url","label":"You still assert the URL","feedback":"The expect(page).to_have_url(...) line has gone missing. Move it, do not delete it."},{"kind":"action","method":"click","locatorKind":"role","label":"You click the Products link","feedback":"No click reached the page. The Act step is what makes this a test of the link rather than a test of the home page."}],"hints":[{"cost":3,"label":"Nudge","text":"Read the failure. It says the URL it found was the home page. When was the assertion checked - before or after the click?"},{"cost":5,"label":"Strategy","text":"Python runs top to bottom. Swap the ASSERT block and the ACT block so the check happens after the thing it is checking."},{"cost":8,"label":"Solution","text":"Move `page.get_by_role(\"link\", name=\"Products\").click()` above the `expect(page).to_have_url(...)` line."}],"reveal":"<p>An assertion that runs before the action is not a strict test - it is a test of the wrong moment. Playwright&#x27;s auto-waiting will not save you here either: <code>to_have_url</code> retried for five seconds on a URL that was never going to change.</p><p>When a test fails, the first question is always <strong>when did it check?</strong></p>"},{"id":"read-the-failure","title":"Read the failure","kind":"predict-the-error","xp":25,"brief":"<p>Failure messages are the most useful documentation you will ever be handed, and beginners skim them. Slow down and read these three properly.</p>","questions":[{"prompt":"<p>A test fails with this. What is the most likely cause?</p>","code":"Locator.click: Timeout 30000ms exceeded.\nCall log:\n  - waiting for get_by_role(\"button\", name=\"Continue Shopping\")","options":["The button was there but disabled","Playwright never found a button with that accessible name","The network was slow","The assertion was wrong"],"answer":1,"why":"\"waiting for <locator>\" with nothing after it means the element never turned up at all. Playwright kept looking for the full 30 seconds. Check the name (it is matched against the accessible name, not the id) and check that the thing you expected to open actually opened."},{"prompt":"<p>And this one - what does the extra line change?</p>","code":"Locator.click: Timeout 30000ms exceeded.\nCall log:\n  - waiting for locator(\"#cartModal\").get_by_role(\"button\", name=\"Continue Shopping\")\n  - locator resolved to a hidden element\n  - retrying click action","options":["Same thing - the element is missing","The element exists but is not visible, so the click was never safe to make","The selector has a syntax error","Playwright clicked it and the click did nothing"],"answer":1,"why":"\"resolved to a hidden element\" means the locator matched - your selector is fine. The element was in the DOM but had no visible box, typically because a modal is still animating in. Playwright refuses to click what a user could not click, and retries instead. Waiting for the modal to be visible first is exactly what pages/products_page.py does in the kit."},{"prompt":"<p>This one is not a timeout. What went wrong?</p>","code":"Locator.click: Error: strict mode violation: get_by_role(\"link\", name=\"Test Cases\")\nresolved to 2 elements:\n    1) <a.test-cases-cta> Test Cases\n    2) <a> Test Cases","options":["The page loaded twice","Your locator is ambiguous - it matches two elements, and Playwright will not guess","You need a longer timeout","One of the two links is broken"],"answer":1,"why":"Strict mode is a feature. Two matches means \"which one did you mean?\" and Playwright fails immediately rather than clicking a coin-flip. Fix it by narrowing: .first, .nth(i), or - better - scope the search to the region you meant, like page.locator(\".shop-menu\").get_by_role(...). Retrying would never help, which is why this one fails instantly instead of after 30 seconds."}],"reveal":"<p>Three shapes, three different first moves:</p><ul><li><strong>&quot;waiting for &lt;locator&gt;&quot;</strong> alone -&gt; the element is not there. Check the name.</li><li><strong>&quot;resolved to a hidden element&quot;</strong> -&gt; your locator is right, your timing is not.</li><li><strong>&quot;strict mode violation&quot;</strong> -&gt; your locator is ambiguous. Narrow it.</li></ul><p>You will meet all three again in the Locator Forest.</p>"},{"id":"follow-the-menu","title":"Follow the menu","kind":"write-the-test","xp":35,"brief":"<p>Write the whole test this time. Starting on the home page:</p><ol><li>click the <strong>Products</strong> link in the menu</li><li>check the address is <code>https://automationexercise.com/products</code></li><li>check the <strong>All Products</strong> heading is on screen</li></ol><p>Two assertions, because they prove different things: one that you <em>navigated</em>, one that the page actually <em>rendered</em>.</p><p>For the heading, <code>get_by_role(&quot;heading&quot;, name=&quot;All Products&quot;)</code> reads better than any CSS selector - it finds it the way a person would.</p>","scenario":{"route":"/"},"starter":"from playwright.sync_api import Page, expect\n\n\ndef test_products_page_opens_from_the_menu(page: Page):\n    page.goto(\"https://automationexercise.com/\")\n\n    # Your turn: click, then check twice.\n","solution":"from playwright.sync_api import Page, expect\n\n\ndef test_products_page_opens_from_the_menu(page: Page):\n    page.goto(\"https://automationexercise.com/\")\n\n    page.get_by_role(\"link\", name=\"Products\").click()\n\n    expect(page).to_have_url(\"https://automationexercise.com/products\")\n    expect(page.get_by_role(\"heading\", name=\"All Products\")).to_be_visible()\n","rubric":["test-function","has-assertion","no-xpath"],"checks":[{"kind":"state","path":"route","equals":"/products","label":"The shop ends up on the products page","feedback":"The shop is still somewhere else. Did the click find the Products link in the menu?"},{"kind":"matcher","name":"to_have_url","label":"You assert the address","feedback":"No to_have_url check ran. Remember it goes on the page: expect(page).to_have_url(...)."},{"kind":"matcher","name":"to_be_visible","label":"You assert the heading is visible","feedback":"No to_be_visible check ran. If your assertion is below a line that failed, it never got the chance - fix the first failure and run again."},{"kind":"locatorKind","name":"role","atLeast":2,"label":"You used role-based locators (the top of the priority list)","feedback":"You got there, but with a CSS selector where a role locator would do. get_by_role finds elements the way a user does, so it survives redesigns that break a class name. Try get_by_role(\"link\", name=\"Products\") and get_by_role(\"heading\", name=\"All Products\")."}],"hints":[{"cost":4,"label":"Nudge","text":"Three lines: one click, two expects. The click is a link; the heading is a heading."},{"cost":6,"label":"Strategy","text":"page.get_by_role(\"link\", name=\"Products\").click(), then expect(page).to_have_url(\"https://automationexercise.com/products\"), then expect(page.get_by_role(\"heading\", name=\"All Products\")).to_be_visible()."}],"reveal":"<p>Notice you never told Playwright to <em>wait</em> for the products page. <code>to_have_url</code> and <code>to_be_visible</code> retry on their own until the page catches up.</p><p>That is the habit this whole quest is built around, and it is why the kit&#x27;s rule is: **never <code>time.sleep()</code>**.</p>"},{"id":"boss-signup-page","title":"Boss: prove the signup page opens","kind":"write-the-test","boss":true,"xp":60,"brief":"<p>No starter code, no comments to fill in. Write the test.</p><p><strong>The behaviour to prove:</strong> from the home page, clicking <strong>Signup / Login</strong> opens the login page, and the <strong>New User Signup!</strong> form is there when it does.</p><p>A reviewer will expect:</p><ul><li>a test function named after the behaviour, not after the page</li><li>the browser tab as the <code>page</code> argument</li><li>an assertion on the address <em>and</em> one on what is on screen</li><li>role-based locators where the element has a visible name</li></ul><p>This is step 3 of <code>learn/test_tc01_register_user.py</code>, written from scratch.</p>","scenario":{"route":"/"},"starter":"from playwright.sync_api import Page, expect\n\n\n","solution":"from playwright.sync_api import Page, expect\n\n\ndef test_signup_login_link_opens_the_signup_form(page: Page):\n    page.goto(\"https://automationexercise.com/\")\n\n    page.get_by_role(\"link\", name=\"Signup / Login\").click()\n\n    expect(page).to_have_url(\"https://automationexercise.com/login\")\n    expect(page.get_by_text(\"New User Signup!\")).to_be_visible()\n","rubric":["test-function","has-assertion","no-xpath","expect-over-assert"],"checks":[{"kind":"state","path":"route","equals":"/login","label":"Your test reaches the login page","feedback":"The shop did not end up on /login. The menu link is labelled \"Signup / Login\" - spaces and slash included."},{"kind":"action","method":"click","label":"You get there by clicking, not by navigating straight to the URL","feedback":"You went straight to /login with goto(). That tests the page, but not the LINK - and the link is the behaviour we are proving. Start at the home page and click."},{"kind":"matcher","name":"to_have_url","label":"You assert the address"},{"kind":"matcher","name":"to_be_visible","label":"You assert the signup form is on screen","feedback":"Nothing checked that the form rendered. A URL can be right while the page behind it is empty - that is a real failure mode, and worth one more line."}],"hints":[{"cost":6,"label":"Nudge","text":"Same shape as the last challenge: goto, click, two expects. The only new thing is that you write the def line yourself."},{"cost":10,"label":"Strategy","text":"def test_signup_login_link_opens_the_signup_form(page: Page): then goto the home page, click get_by_role(\"link\", name=\"Signup / Login\"), then assert the URL is .../login and that get_by_text(\"New User Signup!\") is visible."}],"reveal":"<p>Base Camp cleared.</p><p>You can now write a test that a reviewer would merge: named for its behaviour, arranged-acted-asserted in order, waiting properly, and reading like a sentence.</p><p>Next: <strong>The Locator Forest</strong>, where the interesting question stops being &quot;what do I check&quot; and becomes &quot;how do I find it&quot;.</p>"}]},{"id":"locator-forest","order":1,"badge":"1","abbr":"LF","title":"The Locator Forest","tagline":"Finding the right element among hundreds - and only that one.","teaches":"Locator priority order, chaining, filtering, strict mode, actionability","source":["guide §6 Locators","learn/test_tc07","learn/test_tc09","pages/base_page.py"],"requires":["base-camp"],"map":{"x":22,"y":15},"objectives":["Choose a locator from the priority order instead of grabbing the first thing that works","Scope a search to part of the page by chaining locators","Narrow a list with .filter(), .first and .nth()","Recognise a strict-mode violation and fix the cause rather than the symptom","Explain why Playwright refuses to click an element that something is covering"],"plannedChallenges":[],"lesson":"<h3>A locator is an address, not a search result</h3><pre class=\"lang-python\"><code># Nothing happens yet. This is just the address.\nlogin_button = page.get_by_role(&quot;button&quot;, name=&quot;Login&quot;)\n\n# NOW Playwright goes and finds it.\nlogin_button.click()</code></pre><p>Locators are <strong>lazy</strong> and <strong>self-updating</strong>: every action re-finds the element from scratch. That is why a locator keeps working on a page that redraws itself, and why you can build one before the element exists.</p><h3>The priority order</h3><p>Not all addresses age equally well. Work down this list and stop at the first one that fits:</p><ol><li><code>get_by_role()</code> - what the element <strong>is</strong> plus what it <strong>says</strong></li><li>the other <code>get_by_*</code> methods - label, placeholder, text, alt text</li><li><code>get_by_test_id()</code> - a <code>data-testid</code> the developers added for you</li><li>simple CSS - <code>#search_product</code>, <code>.productinfo</code></li><li>XPath - last resort</li></ol><p>The reason is not taste. The top of the list describes the page the way a <strong>user</strong> perceives it, and that changes far less often than the HTML underneath it. A <code>div.col-sm-4 &gt; div:nth-child(2) &gt; a</code> breaks the day somebody adds a wrapper. &quot;The link called Cart&quot; does not.</p><div class=\"callout note\"><strong>What get_by_role actually matches</strong><p>The <strong>role</strong> is what the element is: <code>button</code>, <code>link</code>, <code>heading</code>, <code>checkbox</code>, <code>textbox</code>. The <strong>name</strong> is its accessible name - usually its visible text, or an <code>aria-label</code>, or the <code>&lt;label&gt;</code> tied to an input. Matching is case-insensitive and matches a substring, so <code>name=&quot;Cart&quot;</code> also matches a link that says &quot;View Cart&quot;. That bites people, and it is Challenge 3.</p></div><h3>When several things match: strict mode</h3><pre class=\"lang-python\"><code>page.get_by_role(&quot;link&quot;, name=&quot;Test Cases&quot;).click()\n# Error: strict mode violation: ... resolved to 2 elements</code></pre><p>Playwright will not guess. Two matches is an error, immediately - no retry, because retrying cannot help. You have three ways out, best first:</p><pre class=\"lang-python\"><code># 1. Scope it: search inside the part of the page you meant\npage.locator(&quot;.shop-menu&quot;).get_by_role(&quot;link&quot;, name=&quot;Cart&quot;).click()\n\n# 2. Filter it: keep only matches containing some text\npage.locator(&quot;.productinfo&quot;).filter(has_text=&quot;Blue Top&quot;)\n\n# 3. Index it: pick by position - correct, but brittle\npage.get_by_role(&quot;link&quot;, name=&quot;Test Cases&quot;).first</code></pre><p><code>.first</code> is not shameful; <code>learn/test_tc07_test_cases_page.py</code> uses it and says why in a comment. But reach for it <em>after</em> you have decided that position is genuinely the thing you mean.</p><h3>Actionability: why a click can time out on a visible element</h3><p>Before Playwright clicks, it checks the element is visible, enabled, and that nothing is on top of it. If a cookie banner is covering your button, you get:</p><pre class=\"lang-text\"><code>- &lt;div class=&quot;consent-popup&quot;&gt; intercepts pointer events\n- retrying click action</code></pre><p>That is Playwright refusing to do something a real user could not do. It is a feature, and it is the reason <code>pages/base_page.py</code> in the kit dismisses the consent popup before doing anything else.</p><h3>Chaining</h3><pre class=\"lang-python\"><code># Search INSIDE another element\npage.locator(&quot;#cartModal&quot;).get_by_role(&quot;button&quot;, name=&quot;Continue Shopping&quot;)\n\n# Keep only matches that contain some text\npage.get_by_role(&quot;listitem&quot;).filter(has_text=&quot;Blue Top&quot;)\n\n# Position, counting from zero\npage.locator(&quot;.productinfo&quot;).nth(2)</code></pre><p>Chaining is the tool you should reach for most, because it says something true about the page: <em>this button, the one inside the modal</em>.</p>","challenges":[{"id":"meet-get-by-role","title":"Meet get_by_role","kind":"locator-match","xp":25,"brief":"<p>Write a locator that matches <strong>exactly one</strong> element: the <strong>Products</strong> link in the shop&#x27;s menu.</p><p>Type only the part after <code>page.</code> - the site wraps it in a real test that asserts the match count, and outlines whatever you matched in the shop panel.</p><p>Start with the top of the priority order. A link&#x27;s role is <code>link</code>; its name is the text you can see.</p>","scenario":{"route":"/"},"expectCount":1,"starter":"","placeholder":"get_by_role(\"link\", name=\"Products\")","solution":"get_by_role(\"link\", name=\"Products\")","rubric":[],"checks":[{"kind":"locatorKind","name":"role","label":"You used get_by_role - the top of the priority order","feedback":"It matched, but with something other than a role locator. A CSS selector like `.shop-menu a:nth-child(2)` is tied to the page's shape; `get_by_role(\"link\", name=\"Products\")` is tied to what a user sees. Try the role version here."},{"kind":"matcher","name":"to_have_count","label":"It matches exactly one element"}],"hints":[{"cost":3,"label":"Nudge","text":"get_by_role takes the role first and the visible name second: get_by_role(\"role\", name=\"...\")."},{"cost":5,"label":"Solution","text":"get_by_role(\"link\", name=\"Products\")"}],"reveal":"<p>Notice what you did <strong>not</strong> have to know: no id, no class, no position in the menu. You described the element the way a person would, and that description survives redesigns.</p><p>Now one trap worth meeting before the next challenge. Try <code>get_by_role(&quot;link&quot;, name=&quot;Cart&quot;)</code> in the same box and watch the count come back as <strong>7</strong>, not 1.</p><p>Name matching finds a <strong>substring</strong>, so <code>&quot;Cart&quot;</code> matches the menu&#x27;s <em>Cart</em> link and every <em>Add to cart</em> link on the page. That is real Playwright behaviour, not a quirk of this shop - and <code>exact=True</code> is how you turn it off when you need to.</p>"},{"id":"strict-mode","title":"Strict mode strikes","kind":"fix-the-broken-test","xp":35,"brief":"<p>Run this before you change anything. It fails instantly - no 30-second wait - and the message tells you why.</p><p>The home page has <strong>two</strong> links called <em>Test Cases</em>: one in the menu, one as a big button in the banner. That is not a bug in the shop; the real automationexercise.com does the same, which is why <code>learn/test_tc07_test_cases_page.py</code> has a comment about it.</p><p>Make the test pass. The menu link and the banner button go to the same place, so either will do - just say which one you mean.</p>","scenario":{"route":"/"},"starter":"from playwright.sync_api import Page, expect\n\n\ndef test_test_cases_page_opens(page: Page):\n    page.goto(\"https://automationexercise.com/\")\n\n    page.get_by_role(\"link\", name=\"Test Cases\").click()\n\n    expect(page).to_have_url(\"https://automationexercise.com/test_cases\")\n    expect(page.get_by_text(\"Below is the list of test Cases\")).to_be_visible()\n","solution":"from playwright.sync_api import Page, expect\n\n\ndef test_test_cases_page_opens(page: Page):\n    page.goto(\"https://automationexercise.com/\")\n\n    # .first because the page has more than one 'Test Cases' link\n    # (one in the menu, one big button in the banner).\n    page.get_by_role(\"link\", name=\"Test Cases\").first.click()\n\n    expect(page).to_have_url(\"https://automationexercise.com/test_cases\")\n    expect(page.get_by_text(\"Below is the list of test Cases\")).to_be_visible()\n","rubric":["test-function","has-assertion"],"checks":[{"kind":"state","path":"route","equals":"/test_cases","label":"The click lands on the test cases page","feedback":"The shop never got there. If you are still seeing a strict mode violation, the locator is still matching both links."},{"kind":"matcher","name":"to_be_visible","label":"The page content assertion still runs"}],"hints":[{"cost":4,"label":"Nudge","text":"The error says \"resolved to 2 elements\". You do not need a different locator - you need to say which of the two."},{"cost":6,"label":"Strategy","text":"Three ways: .first, .nth(0), or scope the search to the menu with page.locator(\".shop-menu\").get_by_role(...). All three are legitimate here."},{"cost":9,"label":"Solution","text":"page.get_by_role(\"link\", name=\"Test Cases\").first.click()"}],"reveal":"<p>Strict mode looks like an obstacle and is actually a gift. The alternative - most other tools&#x27; behaviour - is silently clicking the first match, which means your test passes today and clicks the wrong thing next month when the order changes.</p><p>A failure you can read beats a pass you cannot trust.</p>"},{"id":"scope-the-search","title":"Scope the search","kind":"write-the-test","xp":40,"brief":"<p>You are on the products page. Write a test that:</p><ol><li>adds the <strong>first</strong> product to the cart</li><li>waits for the <em>Added!</em> pop-up to appear</li><li>opens the cart using the <strong>View Cart</strong> link <strong>inside that pop-up</strong></li><li>checks the address is <code>https://automationexercise.com/view_cart</code></li></ol><p>Step 3 is the hard one, and it is hard for two reasons at once. Try the obvious thing first and read what Playwright tells you - both failures are worth meeting.</p><div class=\"callout tip\"><strong>Remember</strong><p>Name matching is a substring, and while the pop-up is open there is a <em>Cart</em> link in the menu <strong>and</strong> a <em>View Cart</em> link in the pop-up.</p></div>","scenario":{"route":"/products"},"starter":"from playwright.sync_api import Page, expect\n\n\ndef test_view_cart_from_the_added_popup(page: Page):\n    page.goto(\"https://automationexercise.com/products\")\n\n    # 1. add the first product\n\n    # 2. wait for the pop-up\n\n    # 3. click View Cart INSIDE the pop-up\n\n    # 4. check where you ended up\n","solution":"from playwright.sync_api import Page, expect\n\n\ndef test_view_cart_from_the_added_popup(page: Page):\n    page.goto(\"https://automationexercise.com/products\")\n\n    page.get_by_role(\"link\", name=\"Add to cart\").first.click()\n\n    modal = page.locator(\"#cartModal\")\n    expect(modal).to_be_visible()\n\n    modal.get_by_role(\"link\", name=\"Cart\").click()\n\n    expect(page).to_have_url(\"https://automationexercise.com/view_cart\")\n","rubric":["test-function","has-assertion","expect-over-assert"],"checks":[{"kind":"state","path":"cartCount","equals":1,"label":"Exactly one product is in the cart","feedback":"The cart does not hold exactly one product. There are eight 'Add to cart' links on this page, so an unqualified locator is a strict-mode violation - add .first."},{"kind":"action","method":"click","targetContains":"#cartModal","label":"You scoped the Cart link to the pop-up","feedback":"You got to the cart, but not by scoping. If you used .last you relied on the pop-up being the last thing in the document - true today, and one layout change from being false. Say what you mean instead: page.locator(\"#cartModal\").get_by_role(\"link\", name=\"Cart\")."},{"kind":"matcher","name":"to_be_visible","label":"You wait for the pop-up before clicking into it","feedback":"No to_be_visible check ran. The pop-up takes 300 ms to appear; without that wait you are clicking at a moment that may or may not exist yet."},{"kind":"state","path":"route","equals":"/view_cart","label":"You end up on the cart page"}],"hints":[{"cost":5,"label":"Nudge","text":"Two separate problems. Adding: eight links say 'Add to cart', so pick one. Opening the cart: two links match name=\"Cart\", and the one in the menu is underneath the pop-up's grey backdrop."},{"cost":8,"label":"Strategy","text":"Give the pop-up a name: modal = page.locator(\"#cartModal\"). Wait for it with expect(modal).to_be_visible(). Then search INSIDE it: modal.get_by_role(\"link\", name=\"Cart\").click()."},{"cost":12,"label":"Solution","text":"page.get_by_role(\"link\", name=\"Add to cart\").first.click(); modal = page.locator(\"#cartModal\"); expect(modal).to_be_visible(); modal.get_by_role(\"link\", name=\"Cart\").click(); expect(page).to_have_url(\"https://automationexercise.com/view_cart\")"}],"reveal":"<p>Two lessons in one challenge.</p><p><strong>Ambiguity is fixed by saying what you mean.</strong> <code>.first</code> and <code>.last</code> work by position; scoping works by meaning. <code>pages/base_page.py</code> in the kit does exactly this, with a comment explaining the same trap:</p><pre class=\"lang-python\"><code>self.page.locator(&quot;.shop-menu&quot;).get_by_role(&quot;link&quot;, name=&quot;Cart&quot;).click()</code></pre><p><strong>Playwright will not click through an overlay.</strong> If you tried the menu link, you got <code>intercepts pointer events</code> rather than a click that silently went to the backdrop. A real user could not have clicked it either.</p>"},{"id":"filter-to-one","title":"Filter the list down","kind":"locator-match","xp":30,"brief":"<p>Eight products are on screen. Two of them are dresses.</p><p>Write one locator that matches <strong>exactly the two dress cards</strong> - not the eight cards, not the two <code>&lt;p&gt;</code> tags holding their names, the two <code>.productinfo</code> cards themselves.</p><p>The tool for this is <code>.filter(has_text=&quot;...&quot;)</code>: start from all the cards, then keep only the ones containing some text.</p>","scenario":{"route":"/products"},"expectCount":2,"starter":"","placeholder":"locator(\".productinfo\").filter(has_text=\"...\")","solution":"locator(\".productinfo\").filter(has_text=\"Dress\")","rubric":[],"checks":[{"kind":"matcher","name":"to_have_count","label":"It matches exactly the two dress cards"},{"kind":"locatorKind","name":"filter","label":"You narrowed the list with .filter()","feedback":"You landed on two elements, but not by filtering. `.nth(2)` and friends depend on the catalogue never being reordered; `.filter(has_text=\"Dress\")` says what you actually mean and keeps working when a ninth product arrives."}],"hints":[{"cost":4,"label":"Nudge","text":"Start from the whole set - locator(\".productinfo\") gives you all eight - then narrow it."},{"cost":6,"label":"Strategy","text":"Both dresses have \"Dress\" in their names: Sleeveless Dress and Stylish Dress. has_text matches a substring and ignores case."},{"cost":9,"label":"Solution","text":"locator(\".productinfo\").filter(has_text=\"Dress\")"}],"reveal":"<p><code>.filter()</code> is the one narrowing tool that describes <strong>meaning</strong> rather than <strong>position</strong>, which is why it survives changes that break <code>.nth()</code>.</p><p>Watch the outlines in the shop panel: the whole card is highlighted, not just the name. Filtering keeps the elements you started from - it does not descend into them.</p>"},{"id":"dismiss-the-blocker","title":"Dismiss the blocker","kind":"fix-the-broken-test","xp":40,"brief":"<p>A cookie-consent panel is covering the shop. This test tries to click straight past it.</p><p><strong>Run it first.</strong> It takes a moment - Playwright retries for the full 30 seconds before giving up - and the call log tells you precisely what stopped it.</p><p>Then fix it by dismissing the panel first. The real site does the same thing, and so does <code>pages/base_page.py</code>:</p><pre class=\"lang-python\"><code>consent_button = self.page.get_by_role(&quot;button&quot;, name=&quot;Consent&quot;)\nif consent_button.count() &gt; 0 and consent_button.first.is_visible():\n    consent_button.first.click()</code></pre><p>Here the panel is always there, so you can just click it - no <code>if</code> needed.</p>","scenario":{"route":"/","consent":true},"starter":"from playwright.sync_api import Page, expect\n\n\ndef test_products_page_opens_past_the_consent_panel(page: Page):\n    page.goto(\"https://automationexercise.com/\")\n\n    page.get_by_role(\"link\", name=\"Products\").click()\n\n    expect(page.get_by_role(\"heading\", name=\"All Products\")).to_be_visible()\n","solution":"from playwright.sync_api import Page, expect\n\n\ndef test_products_page_opens_past_the_consent_panel(page: Page):\n    page.goto(\"https://automationexercise.com/\")\n\n    # Nothing on the page is clickable until this is out of the way.\n    page.get_by_role(\"button\", name=\"Consent\").click()\n\n    page.get_by_role(\"link\", name=\"Products\").click()\n\n    expect(page.get_by_role(\"heading\", name=\"All Products\")).to_be_visible()\n","rubric":["test-function","has-assertion"],"checks":[{"kind":"state","path":"consentOpen","isFalse":true,"label":"The consent panel has been dismissed","feedback":"The panel is still up at the end of your test. Click the Consent button before anything else - while it is open, every click underneath it is intercepted."},{"kind":"state","path":"route","equals":"/products","label":"The Products link click got through"},{"kind":"matcher","name":"to_be_visible","label":"You still assert the heading rendered"}],"hints":[{"cost":5,"label":"Nudge","text":"Read the call log: \"<div class=\"consent-popup\"> intercepts pointer events\". Playwright found your link. Something was on top of it."},{"cost":7,"label":"Strategy","text":"Add one line before the Products click: click the button whose accessible name is Consent. Its role is button, not link."},{"cost":10,"label":"Solution","text":"page.get_by_role(\"button\", name=\"Consent\").click() as the first action after goto."}],"reveal":"<p>This is the failure that makes people add <code>time.sleep(5)</code> and move on - and the sleep never helps, because time was never the problem.</p><p>Read the call log instead. <code>intercepts pointer events</code> names the element that was in the way, and the fix is always the same: deal with the thing in the way.</p><p>It is also why the kit&#x27;s <code>BasePage.open()</code> dismisses consent as part of <em>opening a page</em> rather than leaving it to every test.</p>"},{"id":"priority-order","title":"Which locator would you approve?","kind":"quiz","xp":20,"brief":"<p>You are reviewing a colleague&#x27;s pull request. Four locators, four judgement calls.</p>","questions":[{"prompt":"<p>The page has <code>&lt;button class=&quot;btn btn-primary&quot; data-qa=&quot;login-button&quot;&gt;Login&lt;/button&gt;</code>. Which locator would you ask for?</p>","options":["page.locator(\".btn.btn-primary\")","page.get_by_role(\"button\", name=\"Login\")","page.locator(\"//button[@data-qa='login-button']\")","page.locator(\"button\").nth(3)"],"answer":1,"why":"It has a visible name, so the role locator wins: it is the only one of the four that still works after a redesign, and the only one a reader understands without opening the page. The data-qa attribute is a fine second choice - page.locator('[data-qa=\"login-button\"]') - and the kit uses it for inputs that have no visible label. The class selector matches every primary button on the page, and .nth(3) is a bug waiting for someone to add a button."},{"prompt":"<p>A search box renders as <code>&lt;input type=&quot;text&quot; id=&quot;search_product&quot; placeholder=&quot;Search Product&quot;&gt;</code> with no <code>&lt;label&gt;</code>. The kit uses <code>page.locator(&quot;#search_product&quot;)</code>. Is that a problem?</p>","options":["Yes - CSS is always worse than get_by_*","No - with no label, an id is a reasonable, stable address","Yes - it must be an XPath for inputs","No, but only because the id starts with 'search'"],"answer":1,"why":"The priority order is a default, not a law. An id is stable, unique and readable, and this input has no accessible name to grab. get_by_placeholder(\"Search Product\") is also good here. What you should not do is invent an aria-label in your test's imagination and then wonder why nothing matches."},{"prompt":"<p><code>page.get_by_role(&quot;link&quot;, name=&quot;Cart&quot;)</code> fails with a strict mode violation because a pop-up contains a &quot;View Cart&quot; link. Best fix?</p>","options":["Add .first","Use exact=True so it only matches the exact text \"Cart\"","Scope the search: page.locator(\".shop-menu\").get_by_role(\"link\", name=\"Cart\")","Wait for the pop-up to close with a sleep, then click"],"answer":2,"why":"Scoping is best because it states the intention: the Cart link in the menu. exact=True also fixes this particular clash and is a perfectly good tool - but it fixes the symptom (the substring match) rather than saying which region you meant, and it breaks if the menu ever says \"Cart (2)\". .first depends on document order. The sleep is wrong for all the usual reasons, and would not help anyway - the menu link is under the pop-up's backdrop."},{"prompt":"<p>When is <code>page.locator(&quot;//div[@class=&#x27;features_items&#x27;]//p&quot;)</code> the right call?</p>","options":["Never - XPath is banned","When you need to select by text content, which CSS cannot do","When nothing higher on the priority list fits and CSS cannot express the relationship - and then with a comment saying why","Whenever the element has no id"],"answer":2,"why":"XPath is a legitimate last resort, not a sin - it can walk upwards and match on text, which CSS cannot. The rule is simply that it is last, and that you leave a comment explaining what forced you there. (This particular one is a plain CSS selector wearing a costume: '.features_items p' says the same thing more clearly.)"}],"reveal":"<p>The priority order is about <strong>how long the test survives</strong>, not about purity. Ask one question of any locator you review:</p><p>&gt; If a developer redesigns this page next month without changing what it does, does this still work?</p><p>Role and label locators usually survive. Positions and class names usually do not.</p>"},{"id":"boss-five-ways","title":"Boss: five ways to find a thing","kind":"write-the-test","boss":true,"xp":70,"brief":"<p>One test, five assertions, and <strong>five different locator strategies</strong> - one each. No starter code.</p><p>On the products page, prove that:</p><ol><li>the <strong>All Products</strong> heading is visible — by <em>role</em></li><li>the search box is visible — by <em>placeholder</em></li><li><strong>Blue Top</strong> is on the page — by <em>text</em></li><li>there are exactly <strong>8</strong> product cards — by <em>CSS class</em></li><li>the <strong>third</strong> card is the <em>Sleeveless Dress</em> — by <em>position</em>, chained onto the CSS locator</li></ol><p>For 5, <code>.nth(2)</code> is the third card (counting starts at zero) and <code>to_contain_text</code> is friendlier than <code>to_have_text</code>, because the card also holds a price.</p><p>This is the zone&#x27;s whole toolkit in one function. A reviewer would want each line to use the <em>simplest</em> address that could work.</p>","scenario":{"route":"/products"},"starter":"from playwright.sync_api import Page, expect\n\n\n","solution":"from playwright.sync_api import Page, expect\n\n\ndef test_the_products_page_shows_the_catalogue(page: Page):\n    page.goto(\"https://automationexercise.com/products\")\n\n    # 1. by role\n    expect(page.get_by_role(\"heading\", name=\"All Products\")).to_be_visible()\n\n    # 2. by placeholder\n    expect(page.get_by_placeholder(\"Search Product\")).to_be_visible()\n\n    # 3. by text\n    expect(page.get_by_text(\"Blue Top\")).to_be_visible()\n\n    # 4. by CSS class\n    cards = page.locator(\".productinfo\")\n    expect(cards).to_have_count(8)\n\n    # 5. by position, chained onto the CSS locator\n    expect(cards.nth(2)).to_contain_text(\"Sleeveless Dress\")\n","rubric":["test-function","has-assertion","expect-over-assert","no-xpath"],"checks":[{"kind":"locatorKind","name":"role","label":"You used a role locator","feedback":"No get_by_role in the run. The heading is the one to use it on: get_by_role(\"heading\", name=\"All Products\")."},{"kind":"locatorKind","name":"placeholder","label":"You used a placeholder locator","feedback":"No get_by_placeholder in the run. The search box has no label, so its grey hint text is the best handle it has: get_by_placeholder(\"Search Product\")."},{"kind":"locatorKind","name":"text","label":"You used a text locator","feedback":"No get_by_text in the run. get_by_text(\"Blue Top\") matches the smallest element containing that text - the product name itself."},{"kind":"locatorKind","name":"css","label":"You used a CSS locator","feedback":"No CSS locator in the run. The product cards have no role and no unique text, which is exactly the case CSS is for: locator(\".productinfo\")."},{"kind":"locatorKind","name":"nth","label":"You picked one card by position","feedback":"No .nth() in the run. Position is the right tool here because 'the third card' is genuinely what the requirement says."},{"kind":"matcher","name":"to_have_count","label":"You assert there are 8 cards"},{"kind":"matcher","name":"to_be_visible","atLeast":3,"label":"Three visibility assertions","feedback":"I counted fewer than three to_be_visible checks. If one of your assertions failed, the ones below it never ran - fix the first failure and run again."},{"kind":"matcher","name":"to_contain_text","label":"You check the third card's name","feedback":"No to_contain_text check ran. to_have_text would fail here: the card holds the price as well as the name, and to_have_text wants the whole string."}],"hints":[{"cost":6,"label":"Nudge","text":"Five assertions, five different locator methods: get_by_role, get_by_placeholder, get_by_text, locator, and .nth() chained onto the last one."},{"cost":10,"label":"Strategy","text":"Give the CSS locator a name - cards = page.locator(\".productinfo\") - and reuse it for both the count and the .nth(2) check. A locator is just an address, so holding on to one costs nothing."}],"reveal":"<p>The Locator Forest is cleared.</p><p>The habit worth taking out of it: <strong>pick the address that describes what the thing is, not where it happens to sit.</strong> Reach down the priority list only when the level above genuinely cannot express what you mean, and leave a note when you do.</p><p>Next: <strong>Assertion Ridge</strong>, where you stop finding elements and start making promises about them - and find out why <code>expect()</code> waits and <code>assert</code> does not.</p>"}]},{"id":"assertion-ridge","order":2,"badge":"2","abbr":"AR","title":"Assertion Ridge","tagline":"Web-first checks that wait for the page instead of racing it.","teaches":"expect(), auto-waiting, choosing a matcher, negation, counts","source":["guide §7 Assertions","learn/test_tc09","learn/test_tc12","learn/test_tc17"],"requires":["locator-forest"],"map":{"x":36,"y":25},"objectives":["Explain why expect() waits and a bare assert does not","Pick the matcher that says what you actually mean","Assert that something is gone, not just that it was never there","Know the one case where a plain assert is still the right tool"],"plannedChallenges":[],"lesson":"<h3>The assertion is the test</h3><p>Everything before it is setup. <code>expect()</code> is the line that can fail, and therefore the line that has value.</p><h3>Web-first means it retries</h3><pre class=\"lang-python\"><code>expect(page.get_by_text(&quot;Order placed!&quot;)).to_be_visible()</code></pre><p>This does not check once. It keeps re-checking - for up to 5 seconds by default - until the condition holds or time runs out. A success message that takes 800 ms to arrive simply passes.</p><p>Compare:</p><pre class=\"lang-python\"><code>assert page.get_by_text(&quot;Order placed!&quot;).is_visible()   # checks ONCE</code></pre><p><code>is_visible()</code> answers <em>&quot;is this true at this exact millisecond?&quot;</em>. Right after a click, the answer is usually no, because the page has not caught up yet. This assert passes on a fast machine and fails on a busy CI runner. That is the definition of a flaky test, and it is the single most common bug in beginners&#x27; suites.</p><div class=\"callout warn\"><strong>Never reach for time.sleep()</strong><p>A fixed sleep is wrong twice: too short on a slow day, so the test fails for no reason, and too long on a fast day, so every run pays for it. <code>expect()</code> waits exactly as long as it needs to. If you are tempted to sleep, there is an <code>expect()</code> that says what you were really waiting for - and this site will not run code containing a sleep, so you may as well find it now.</p></div><h3>Picking a matcher</h3><p>| You mean | Write | |---|---| | it is on screen | <code>to_be_visible()</code> | | its text is exactly this | <code>to_have_text(&quot;Done&quot;)</code> | | its text includes this | <code>to_contain_text(&quot;Don&quot;)</code> | | this input holds this value | <code>to_have_value(&quot;42&quot;)</code> | | exactly N elements match | <code>to_have_count(3)</code> | | it can be clicked | <code>to_be_enabled()</code> | | a checkbox is ticked | <code>to_be_checked()</code> | | the tab title | <code>expect(page).to_have_title(...)</code> | | the address | <code>expect(page).to_have_url(...)</code> |</p><p><code>to_have_text</code> compares the <strong>whole</strong> string, so it fails on a card that also contains a price. <code>to_contain_text</code> is the friendlier default when you only care about part of it.</p><h3>Proving something is gone</h3><pre class=\"lang-python\"><code>expect(page.get_by_text(&quot;Cart is empty!&quot;)).to_be_visible()\nexpect(page.locator(&quot;#product-1&quot;)).not_to_be_visible()\nexpect(page.locator(&quot;#cart_info_table tbody tr&quot;)).to_have_count(0)</code></pre><p>Negative assertions retry too - they wait for the thing to <em>disappear</em>, which is exactly what you need after clicking a delete button.</p><h3>When a plain assert is right</h3><p>When you have already read a value into a variable, there is nothing left to wait for:</p><pre class=\"lang-python\"><code>names = page.locator(&quot;.productinfo p&quot;).all_inner_texts()\ndresses = [name for name in names if &quot;dress&quot; in name.lower()]\nassert len(dresses) &gt; 0, &quot;Expected at least one dress in the results&quot;</code></pre><p>That is straight out of <code>learn/test_tc09_search_product.py</code>, and it is correct. The rule is not &quot;never assert&quot; - it is <strong>never assert on something you should have waited for</strong>.</p><h3>Reading a failure</h3><pre class=\"lang-text\"><code>AssertionError: Locator expected to be visible\nActual value: hidden\nCall log:\n  - expect(locator).to_be_visible with timeout 5000ms\n  - waiting for get_by_text(&quot;Order placed!&quot;)</code></pre><p>What was expected, what was found, how long it tried, and what it was looking for. Four facts, and usually the whole answer.</p>","challenges":[{"id":"expect-waits","title":"expect() waits for you","kind":"write-the-test","xp":30,"brief":"<p>Search the shop for <strong>dress</strong> and prove the results arrived.</p><ol><li>type <code>dress</code> into the search box (<code>#search_product</code> - it has no label, so an id is the right handle)</li><li>click the search button (<code>#submit_search</code>)</li><li>assert the <strong>Searched Products</strong> heading is visible</li></ol><p>The shop takes <strong>600 ms</strong> to answer, and while it is thinking the product grid is empty. You will not need to do anything about that - which is the point of the challenge.</p>","scenario":{"route":"/products"},"starter":"from playwright.sync_api import Page, expect\n\n\ndef test_search_shows_the_results_heading(page: Page):\n    page.goto(\"https://automationexercise.com/products\")\n\n    # 1. type the search term\n\n    # 2. click search\n\n    # 3. assert the results heading appeared\n","solution":"from playwright.sync_api import Page, expect\n\n\ndef test_search_shows_the_results_heading(page: Page):\n    page.goto(\"https://automationexercise.com/products\")\n\n    page.locator(\"#search_product\").fill(\"dress\")\n    page.locator(\"#submit_search\").click()\n\n    expect(page.get_by_role(\"heading\", name=\"Searched Products\")).to_be_visible()\n","rubric":["test-function","has-assertion","expect-over-assert"],"checks":[{"kind":"state","path":"query","equals":"dress","label":"The shop actually ran a search for \"dress\"","feedback":"The shop did not finish a search for \"dress\". Check that you filled the box before clicking, and that your test waited long enough for the results - which expect() does for you."},{"kind":"matcher","name":"to_be_visible","label":"You assert the results heading is visible"},{"kind":"virtualMs","atLeast":600,"label":"Your test waited out the shop's 600 ms response","feedback":"Your test finished before the results could possibly have arrived. That means nothing actually waited - an assertion that passes at 0 ms is not looking at the search results."}],"hints":[{"cost":4,"label":"Nudge","text":".fill(\"dress\") on the box, .click() on the button, then one expect(). No waiting code of any kind."},{"cost":6,"label":"Strategy","text":"The heading is a heading, so get_by_role(\"heading\", name=\"Searched Products\") reads best. Before the search it says \"All Products\"; afterwards it says \"Searched Products\"."},{"cost":9,"label":"Solution","text":"page.locator(\"#search_product\").fill(\"dress\"); page.locator(\"#submit_search\").click(); expect(page.get_by_role(\"heading\", name=\"Searched Products\")).to_be_visible()"}],"reveal":"<p>Look at the <strong>page time used</strong> figure under the results: 600 ms.</p><p>Your test waited exactly as long as the shop took - not a fixed guess, not zero. You wrote no waiting code at all; <code>to_be_visible()</code> retried until the heading turned up.</p><p>That is the whole argument against <code>time.sleep(2)</code>. A sleep would have waited 2000 ms for a 600 ms operation, and would still have been too short the day the shop got slower.</p>"},{"id":"why-the-assert-failed","title":"Why the assert failed","kind":"predict-the-error","xp":25,"brief":"<p>Same search you just wrote, three different ways of checking it. Work out what each one does before you read the options.</p>","questions":[{"prompt":"<p>This fails about nine times out of ten on a busy machine, and passes on a fast one. Why?</p>","code":"page.locator(\"#submit_search\").click()\nassert page.get_by_role(\"heading\", name=\"Searched Products\").is_visible()","options":["is_visible() is broken and should never be used","is_visible() takes one snapshot and never retries, so it looks before the results arrive","The locator is wrong","assert cannot be used with Playwright"],"answer":1,"why":"is_visible() is not broken - it is doing exactly what it says, answering about this instant. The bug is asking the question too early. Every Playwright method whose name starts with is_ or returns a plain value (count(), inner_text()) is a snapshot. Snapshots belong in variables you then reason about, not in the assertion that decides whether the page is ready."},{"prompt":"<p>A colleague fixes the flake like this. What would you say in review?</p>","code":"page.locator(\"#submit_search\").click()\ntime.sleep(3)\nassert page.get_by_role(\"heading\", name=\"Searched Products\").is_visible()","options":["Looks good - three seconds is plenty","It hides the flake rather than fixing it, wastes 2.4 s on every run, and will still fail the day the search takes 3.1 s","Use time.sleep(10) to be safe","Move the sleep before the click"],"answer":1,"why":"It usually passes, which is what makes it dangerous - it looks fixed. Multiply 2.4 wasted seconds by 300 tests and your suite takes ten extra minutes to be no more reliable. expect(...).to_be_visible() waits 600 ms here and would wait 3.1 s if it had to. It is both faster and safer, which is a rare combination."},{"prompt":"<p>This one passes, and it is still wrong. What is it actually testing?</p>","code":"page.locator(\"#submit_search\").click()\nexpect(page.get_by_role(\"heading\", name=\"All Products\")).to_be_visible()","options":["Nothing is wrong - the heading is visible","It asserts the heading BEFORE the search - it passes at 0 ms and would still pass if search were completely broken","to_be_visible cannot be used on headings","It needs a longer timeout"],"answer":1,"why":"A green test that cannot fail is worse than no test, because it buys false confidence. \"All Products\" is what the heading says before the search finishes, so this assertion is satisfied instantly and never looks at the results at all. When you write an assertion, ask: what change to the product would make this go red? If the answer is \"nothing\", it is not a test."}],"reveal":"<p>Three ways to get a wrong answer:</p><ul><li><strong>assert + snapshot</strong> - checks too early, fails randomly</li><li><strong>sleep + assert</strong> - checks late enough today, wastes time forever, still random</li><li><strong>expect on the wrong thing</strong> - never fails at all</li></ul><p>One way to get a right one: <code>expect()</code> on the thing that is only true <strong>after</strong> the behaviour you are testing.</p>"},{"id":"pick-the-matcher","title":"Pick the right matcher","kind":"fill-the-blank","xp":30,"brief":"<p>The cart holds one Blue Top at Rs. 500. The locators are written for you; the matchers are not.</p><p>Replace each <code>REPLACE_ME(...)</code> with the matcher that says what the comment above it means. One of them is a trap: read what the element actually contains before you choose.</p>","scenario":{"route":"/view_cart","cart":[{"id":1,"qty":1}]},"starter":"from playwright.sync_api import Page, expect\n\n\ndef test_the_cart_shows_one_blue_top(page: Page):\n    page.goto(\"https://automationexercise.com/view_cart\")\n\n    # There is exactly one row in the cart table.\n    expect(page.locator(\"#cart_info_table tbody tr\")).REPLACE_ME(1)\n\n    # The quantity cell says exactly \"1\".\n    expect(page.locator(\".cart_quantity\")).REPLACE_ME(\"1\")\n\n    # The price cell mentions rupees somewhere in it.\n    expect(page.locator(\".cart_price\")).REPLACE_ME(\"Rs.\")\n\n    # The checkout link can be clicked.\n    expect(page.get_by_role(\"link\", name=\"Proceed To Checkout\")).REPLACE_ME()\n","solution":"from playwright.sync_api import Page, expect\n\n\ndef test_the_cart_shows_one_blue_top(page: Page):\n    page.goto(\"https://automationexercise.com/view_cart\")\n\n    expect(page.locator(\"#cart_info_table tbody tr\")).to_have_count(1)\n    expect(page.locator(\".cart_quantity\")).to_have_text(\"1\")\n    expect(page.locator(\".cart_price\")).to_contain_text(\"Rs.\")\n    expect(page.get_by_role(\"link\", name=\"Proceed To Checkout\")).to_be_enabled()\n","rubric":["test-function","has-assertion","expect-over-assert"],"checks":[{"kind":"matcher","name":"to_have_count","label":"Row count: to_have_count(1)","feedback":"\"There is exactly one row\" is a count. to_have_count(1) is the only matcher that can express it - and unlike counting by hand it retries while the table renders."},{"kind":"matcher","name":"to_have_text","label":"Quantity: to_have_text(\"1\")","feedback":"\"Says exactly 1\" is to_have_text. to_contain_text(\"1\") would also pass here, and would keep passing if the cell said 21 - so it is the weaker check."},{"kind":"matcher","name":"to_contain_text","label":"Price: to_contain_text(\"Rs.\")","feedback":"This is the trap. The price cell reads \"Rs. 500\", so to_have_text(\"Rs.\") fails - it compares the whole string. When you only care about part of the text, to_contain_text is the honest matcher."},{"kind":"matcher","name":"to_be_enabled","label":"Checkout link: to_be_enabled()","feedback":"\"Can be clicked\" is to_be_enabled(). Note it takes no argument - it is a question about the element, not a comparison."}],"hints":[{"cost":4,"label":"Nudge","text":"Four matchers, all in the table in the lesson above. The trap is the price: look at what the cell really says."},{"cost":7,"label":"Strategy","text":"to_have_count for the rows, to_have_text for the exact quantity, to_contain_text for the price (the cell reads \"Rs. 500\", not \"Rs.\"), to_be_enabled for the link."}],"reveal":"<p>The difference between <code>to_have_text</code> and <code>to_contain_text</code> is where most matcher bugs live.</p><p><code>to_have_text</code> is stricter, so prefer it <strong>when you genuinely know the whole string</strong>. When the element also holds a price, a currency symbol or trailing whitespace you do not control, <code>to_contain_text</code> says what you mean instead of what you hope.</p>"},{"id":"count-them","title":"Count them - and be careful what you promise","kind":"write-the-test","xp":35,"brief":"<p>Search for <strong>women</strong> and check the results. There are <strong>7</strong> matching products.</p><ol><li>search for <code>women</code></li><li>assert there are exactly 7 <code>.productinfo</code> cards</li><li>read every product name with <code>.all_inner_texts()</code> on <code>.productinfo p</code>, print them, and assert that <strong>at least one</strong> name contains &quot;women&quot;</li></ol><p>Step 3 is a plain <code>assert</code>, and that is correct here - by then you are comparing a list you already hold, not waiting for the page.</p><p>Why &quot;at least one&quot; rather than &quot;all of them&quot;? Run it and look at what gets printed. The answer is the point of this challenge.</p>","scenario":{"route":"/products"},"starter":"from playwright.sync_api import Page, expect\n\n\ndef test_searching_for_women_returns_the_womens_range(page: Page):\n    page.goto(\"https://automationexercise.com/products\")\n\n    # 1. run the search\n\n    # 2. assert the number of results\n\n    # 3. read the names, print them, and assert at least one matches\n","solution":"from playwright.sync_api import Page, expect\n\n\ndef test_searching_for_women_returns_the_womens_range(page: Page):\n    page.goto(\"https://automationexercise.com/products\")\n\n    page.locator(\"#search_product\").fill(\"women\")\n    page.locator(\"#submit_search\").click()\n\n    results = page.locator(\".productinfo\")\n    expect(results).to_have_count(7)\n\n    # The search matches the product's CATEGORY as well as its name, so we\n    # can only promise that SOME result mentions women - not all of them.\n    names = page.locator(\".productinfo p\").all_inner_texts()\n    print(f\"Products found: {names}\")\n    matches = [name for name in names if \"women\" in name.lower()]\n    assert len(matches) > 0, \"Expected at least one product with 'women' in its name\"\n","rubric":["test-function","has-assertion","expect-over-assert"],"checks":[{"kind":"state","path":"searchResults","equals":7,"label":"The shop ran the search and found 7 products","feedback":"The shop is not showing 7 results. Check the search term - and remember expect() will wait out the 600 ms response for you."},{"kind":"matcher","name":"to_have_count","label":"You assert the result count with to_have_count","feedback":"No to_have_count check ran. Counting with len(locator.all_inner_texts()) would work but takes a snapshot - to_have_count retries while the results render."},{"kind":"action","method":"all_inner_texts","label":"You read every product name with .all_inner_texts()","feedback":"No all_inner_texts() call reached the page. It returns a plain Python list of strings - one per matching element."},{"kind":"printed","contains":"Blue Top","label":"You printed the names you found","feedback":"Nothing printed containing the product names. print() the list - seeing the real data is the whole reason this challenge exists."}],"hints":[{"cost":5,"label":"Nudge","text":"Same search code as before. Then: expect(page.locator(\".productinfo\")).to_have_count(7), then names = page.locator(\".productinfo p\").all_inner_texts()."},{"cost":8,"label":"Strategy","text":"For the last assert: matches = [name for name in names if \"women\" in name.lower()] then assert len(matches) > 0 with a message explaining what you expected."}],"reveal":"<p>Look at what printed. Seven results, and only <strong>Madame Top For Women</strong> actually has &quot;women&quot; in its name.</p><p>The search matches the <strong>category</strong> too, and every product in <em>Women &gt; Tops</em> and <em>Women &gt; Dress</em> came back. If you had written the obvious-looking assertion -</p><pre class=\"lang-python\"><code>assert all(&quot;women&quot; in name.lower() for name in names)   # WRONG</code></pre><ul><li>you would have written a test that fails on correct behaviour, then &quot;fixed&quot; the product to satisfy it.</li></ul><p>This is the lesson <code>learn/test_tc09_search_product.py</code> stops to make in a comment: <strong>find out how a feature actually behaves before writing a strict assertion about it.</strong> The assertion is a promise, and you should only promise what is true.</p>"},{"id":"not-there-anymore","title":"Prove it is gone","kind":"write-the-test","xp":35,"brief":"<p>The cart holds a <strong>Blue Top</strong> and a <strong>Men Tshirt</strong>. Remove the Blue Top and prove it left.</p><ol><li>click the <strong>X</strong> next to the Blue Top — it is a link, and its accessible name is <code>Remove Blue Top</code></li><li>assert its row (<code>#product-1</code>) is no longer visible</li><li>assert the table is down to <strong>1</strong> row</li></ol><p>Removal takes 300 ms, so the row is still there the instant after you click. Negative assertions retry as well: they wait for the thing to <em>go away</em>.</p>","scenario":{"route":"/view_cart","cart":[{"id":1,"qty":1},{"id":2,"qty":1}]},"starter":"from playwright.sync_api import Page, expect\n\n\ndef test_removing_a_product_takes_it_out_of_the_cart(page: Page):\n    page.goto(\"https://automationexercise.com/view_cart\")\n\n    # Sanity check: we start with two rows.\n    rows = page.locator(\"#cart_info_table tbody tr\")\n    expect(rows).to_have_count(2)\n\n    # 1. remove the Blue Top\n\n    # 2. its row should disappear\n\n    # 3. one row should be left\n","solution":"from playwright.sync_api import Page, expect\n\n\ndef test_removing_a_product_takes_it_out_of_the_cart(page: Page):\n    page.goto(\"https://automationexercise.com/view_cart\")\n\n    rows = page.locator(\"#cart_info_table tbody tr\")\n    expect(rows).to_have_count(2)\n\n    page.get_by_role(\"link\", name=\"Remove Blue Top\").click()\n\n    expect(page.locator(\"#product-1\")).not_to_be_visible()\n    expect(rows).to_have_count(1)\n","rubric":["test-function","has-assertion","expect-over-assert"],"checks":[{"kind":"state","path":"cartCount","equals":1,"label":"One product is left in the cart","feedback":"The cart does not hold exactly one product. Did the X get clicked - and did your test wait the 300 ms for the removal to land?"},{"kind":"state","path":"cartItems.0.name","equals":"Men Tshirt","label":"It is the Blue Top that went, not the other one","feedback":"The wrong product was removed. Each X has its own accessible name: \"Remove Blue Top\" and \"Remove Men Tshirt\"."},{"kind":"matcher","name":"not_to_be_visible","label":"You assert the row is gone with not_to_be_visible()","feedback":"No negative visibility check ran. expect(page.locator(\"#product-1\")).not_to_be_visible() waits for the row to disappear - a plain `assert not ....is_visible()` would check too early and pass for the wrong reason."},{"kind":"matcher","name":"to_have_count","atLeast":2,"label":"You check the row count before and after","feedback":"I only saw one to_have_count. Keep the starting check as well - it proves the test began from the state you thought it did, so a later failure means something real."}],"hints":[{"cost":5,"label":"Nudge","text":"The X links have aria-labels, so get_by_role(\"link\", name=\"Remove Blue Top\") finds exactly the right one without any CSS."},{"cost":8,"label":"Strategy","text":"expect(page.locator(\"#product-1\")).not_to_be_visible() for the row, then expect(rows).to_have_count(1) - you can reuse the `rows` locator from the top, because a locator is only an address."}],"reveal":"<p>Two things worth keeping.</p><p><strong>Negative assertions wait too.</strong> <code>not_to_be_visible()</code> retried for the 300 ms the removal took. <code>assert not row.is_visible()</code> would have passed instantly - and would also have passed if you had clicked the wrong button entirely, because the row would still be there but the assertion would have looked before the page moved.</p><p><strong>Assert your starting state.</strong> The <code>to_have_count(2)</code> at the top costs one line and turns &quot;the test failed&quot; into &quot;the test failed <em>after</em> the setup was correct&quot;.</p>"},{"id":"boss-search-end-to-end","title":"Boss: rebuild Test Case 9","kind":"write-the-test","boss":true,"xp":70,"brief":"<p>Write <code>learn/test_tc09_search_product.py</code> from scratch, without looking at it. No starter code.</p><p><strong>The behaviour to prove:</strong> searching the products page for <em>dress</em> returns dresses.</p><p>Everything a reviewer will look for:</p><ul><li>a test function named after the behaviour</li><li>search for <code>dress</code> from the products page</li><li>the <strong>Searched Products</strong> heading appears</li><li>at least one product card is visible</li><li>exactly <strong>2</strong> cards come back</li><li>the names are read, printed, and checked for at least one containing &quot;dress&quot;</li></ul><p>Every technique in this zone, in one function. When it passes, open <code>learn/test_tc09_search_product.py</code> and compare - you should recognise your own test.</p>","scenario":{"route":"/products"},"starter":"from playwright.sync_api import Page, expect\n\n\n","solution":"from playwright.sync_api import Page, expect\n\n\ndef test_search_for_a_dress(page: Page):\n    page.goto(\"https://automationexercise.com/products\")\n\n    page.locator(\"#search_product\").fill(\"dress\")\n    page.locator(\"#submit_search\").click()\n\n    expect(page.get_by_role(\"heading\", name=\"Searched Products\")).to_be_visible()\n\n    results = page.locator(\".productinfo\")\n    expect(results.first).to_be_visible()\n    expect(results).to_have_count(2)\n\n    names = page.locator(\".productinfo p\").all_inner_texts()\n    print(f\"Products found: {names}\")\n    dresses = [name for name in names if \"dress\" in name.lower()]\n    assert len(dresses) > 0, \"Expected at least one product with 'dress' in its name\"\n","rubric":["test-function","has-assertion","expect-over-assert","no-xpath"],"checks":[{"kind":"state","path":"query","equals":"dress","label":"The shop ran a search for \"dress\"","feedback":"No finished search for \"dress\". Fill #search_product, then click #submit_search."},{"kind":"state","path":"searchResults","equals":2,"label":"Two dresses came back"},{"kind":"matcher","name":"to_be_visible","atLeast":2,"label":"You assert both the heading and the first card are visible","feedback":"I counted fewer than two to_be_visible checks. Both matter, and they say different things: the heading proves the search ran, the card proves it returned something."},{"kind":"matcher","name":"to_have_count","label":"You assert exactly how many results there are","feedback":"No to_have_count check ran. \"Some results\" is a weak promise - say how many, and the test notices when the search starts over-matching."},{"kind":"action","method":"all_inner_texts","label":"You read the product names","feedback":"No all_inner_texts() call reached the page. You need the actual names to check them."},{"kind":"printed","contains":"Dress","label":"You print what you found","feedback":"Nothing was printed containing a product name. A print here is not debugging litter - when this test fails in CI at 3 a.m., the printed list is what tells you whether the search broke or the catalogue changed."},{"kind":"virtualMs","atLeast":600,"label":"Your test waited out the shop's response","feedback":"Your test finished too early for the results to have arrived, so the assertions were not looking at search results at all."}],"hints":[{"cost":7,"label":"Nudge","text":"Six steps, in order: goto, fill, click, expect the heading, expect the cards (visible and count), then read and check the names."},{"cost":12,"label":"Strategy","text":"Hold the cards in a variable: results = page.locator(\".productinfo\"). Then expect(results.first).to_be_visible() and expect(results).to_have_count(2) reuse the same address. .first is needed on the visibility check because eight - well, two - cards match, and strict mode will not pick for you."}],"reveal":"<p>Assertion Ridge is cleared, and you have just reproduced one of the kit&#x27;s 26 real tests.</p><p>The habit to carry forward: <strong>an assertion is a promise about behaviour you have verified, waited for properly, and can defend in review.</strong></p><p>Ahead lie the Form Marshes, Cart Caverns, and eventually Page Object Peaks - where the question becomes how to keep tests this readable once there are two hundred of them. Those zones are mapped but not yet built; <code>site/CONTRIBUTING-CONTENT.md</code> explains how to add one, and it is a single JSON file.</p>"}]},{"id":"form-marshes","order":3,"badge":"3","abbr":"FM","title":"The Form Marshes","tagline":"Filling things in, and proving the page pushed back when it should.","teaches":"fill, check, uncheck, select_option, press, and the negative cases most suites forget","source":["guide §6 Locators","learn/test_tc01_register_user","learn/test_tc05_register_existing_email","learn/test_tc06_contact_us_form","pages/login_page.py"],"requires":["assertion-ridge"],"map":{"x":50,"y":38},"objectives":["Fill text inputs, radios, checkboxes and dropdowns with the method each one actually needs","Reach a field by its visible label instead of by the id a developer happened to choose","Write the negative case: prove the form REFUSES bad input, not just that it accepts good input","Generate your own test data so two runs never collide over the same email address","Explain why select_option() takes a value, a label or an index - and which one survives a redesign"],"plannedChallenges":["Fill the signup form by label","Radios, checkboxes and the difference between check() and click()","Dropdowns: value, label, or index?","Fix the broken test: it fills the box but never submits","The negative case: registering an email that already exists","Data that does not collide: unique_email() and why it exists","Boss: rebuild Test Case 6, the Contact Us form end to end"],"lesson":"","challenges":[]},{"id":"cart-caverns","order":4,"badge":"4","abbr":"CC","title":"Cart Caverns","tagline":"State that survives across pages - and the assertions that pin it down.","teaches":"Multi-step journeys, counting rows, quantities, removal, and asserting a thing is GONE","source":["guide §7 Assertions","learn/test_tc12_add_products_in_cart","learn/test_tc13_product_quantity_in_cart","learn/test_tc17_remove_product_from_cart","pages/cart_page.py"],"requires":["form-marshes"],"map":{"x":63,"y":31},"objectives":["Carry state across three pages without letting the test lose track of what it added","Count rows with to_have_count() instead of reading a number out of the page and trusting it","Assert a row's quantity and price by scoping to that row, not to the whole table","Prove a removal happened with not_to_be_visible() - the check most suites forget","Recognise the moment a test has grown too long to read, which is the door to Zone 6"],"plannedChallenges":["Add one product and prove it landed","Add two, and count the rows","Scope to one row: the right price on the right line","Change the quantity on the product page and check it survived","Fix the broken test: it asserts on the wrong row","Remove it, and prove it is gone","Boss: rebuild Test Case 17, add and remove with both states proved"],"lesson":"","challenges":[]},{"id":"fixture-foundry","order":5,"badge":"5","abbr":"FF","title":"Fixture Foundry","tagline":"Setup and teardown that runs even when the test explodes.","teaches":"conftest.py, fixtures, yield, scope, and why cleanup belongs after the yield","source":["guide §8 Setup and teardown","guide §4 Configurations","fixtures/browser.py","fixtures/reporting.py","conftest.py"],"requires":["cart-caverns"],"map":{"x":77,"y":40},"objectives":["Explain what pytest does when it sees an argument name it recognises as a fixture","Write a fixture that yields, and put the cleanup where a failing test cannot skip it","Choose a scope on purpose - function, module, session - and say what you traded away","Put a fixture in conftest.py so every test in the folder can use it without importing anything","Name the one thing a fixture must never do: leave the next test a page it did not ask for"],"plannedChallenges":["Where does `page` come from?","Your first fixture: a logged-in session","yield, and the cleanup that always runs","Fix the broken test: teardown that never fires because the test failed first","Scope: why session-scoped state is a loaded gun","Move it to conftest.py and delete the import","Boss: build the fixture that creates a user and deletes it afterwards, whatever happens"],"lesson":"","challenges":[]},{"id":"page-object-peaks","order":6,"badge":"6","abbr":"PP","title":"Page Object Peaks","tagline":"Two hundred tests, one place to fix a locator. The climb starts here.","teaches":"The layering the whole kit is built on: tests assert, helpers do journeys, pages own EVERY locator","source":["guide §5 Creating test scripts","pages/base_page.py","pages/products_page.py","helpers/flows.py"],"requires":["fixture-foundry"],"map":{"x":91,"y":58},"objectives":["State the rule in one line: tests assert, helpers run journeys, pages hold all the locators","Move a locator out of a test and give the page-object method a name that reads like intent","Decide what belongs in a page object and what belongs in a helper - and defend the line you drew","Change one id in the markup and fix the whole suite by editing a single file","Recognise the page object that has quietly grown assertions inside it, and take them back out"],"plannedChallenges":["Spot the locator that escaped into the test","Name the method: login(), not click_submit_button()","Fill the blank: the page object's missing method","Should this be a page object or a helper?","The markup changed - fix the suite in one file","The page object that asserts too much","Boss: refactor a 40-line test into pages/ + helpers/ with no locator left behind"],"lesson":"","challenges":[]},{"id":"flaky-swamp","order":7,"badge":"7","abbr":"FS","title":"The Flaky Swamp","tagline":"The test that passes on your machine and fails in CI at 3 a.m.","teaches":"Races, fixed waits, order dependence, and how to debug a failure you cannot reproduce","source":["guide §11 Additional info","guide §7 Assertions","learn/test_tc20_search_and_verify_cart_after_login","pytest.ini"],"requires":["page-object-peaks"],"map":{"x":70,"y":74},"objectives":["Name the four usual causes of a flaky test and spot which one you are looking at","Replace every fixed wait with an assertion that says what you are actually waiting for","Find the test that only passes because the test before it left something behind","Read a Playwright timeout message and get the cause out of it, not just the line number","Argue honestly about --reruns: what it buys, and what it hides"],"plannedChallenges":["Spot the flake: three tests, one of them is lying","Predict the error message","The sleep that works until it does not","The test that only passes second","Read the trace: where did the 30 seconds go?","Fix the race without adding a single wait","Boss: take a suite that fails one run in five and make it fail zero"],"lesson":"","challenges":[]},{"id":"checkout-citadel","order":8,"badge":"8","abbr":"CQ","title":"Checkout Citadel","tagline":"The long journey: register, shop, check out, pay, and clean up after yourself.","teaches":"Full end-to-end flows built from helpers, self-contained data, and teardown that always runs","source":["learn/test_tc14_order_register_while_checkout","learn/test_tc15_order_register_before_checkout","learn/test_tc16_order_login_before_checkout","learn/test_tc24_download_invoice","helpers/flows.py","utils/data.py"],"requires":["flaky-swamp"],"map":{"x":56,"y":76},"objectives":["Build a ten-step journey that still reads like a paragraph, because the steps are helpers","Create the data the test needs instead of depending on data somebody else left behind","Delete the account you created, even when the assertion in the middle failed","Assert at the points that matter, rather than after every single click","Judge when one long E2E test is right and when it should have been three short ones"],"plannedChallenges":["Register, then shop: the journey in helpers","Self-contained data: your own account, every run","Assert at the checkpoints that matter","Fix the broken test: it checks out as the wrong user","Teardown that survives a mid-test failure","One long test or three short ones?","Boss: rebuild Test Case 16 - login, check out, verify the invoice, delete the account"],"lesson":"","challenges":[]},{"id":"runners-gate","order":9,"badge":"9","abbr":"RG","title":"The Runner's Gate","tagline":"A suite nobody can run on demand is a suite nobody runs.","teaches":"Markers, smoke vs regression, selecting tests, HTML and Allure reports, and CI","source":["guide §9 Categorization of tests","guide §10 How to run tests and check reports","pytest.ini","run_tests.py",".github/workflows"],"requires":["checkout-citadel"],"map":{"x":38,"y":72},"objectives":["Tag a test with a marker and register it, so a typo is an error instead of a silent skip","Split a suite into smoke and regression, and defend which tests went where","Select exactly the tests you want with -m and -k, and know which one you reached for","Read an HTML report and an Allure report and say what each is actually for","Explain what changes when the same suite runs in CI instead of on your laptop"],"plannedChallenges":["Tag it: @pytest.mark.smoke","Register the marker, or pay for it later","-m or -k? Pick the right selector","skip, xfail, and the difference that matters","Read the report: which failure do you open first?","Headed on your laptop, headless in CI","Boss: split a 26-test suite into a 3-minute smoke run and a nightly regression run"],"lesson":"","challenges":[]},{"id":"desktop-outpost","order":10,"badge":"★","abbr":"DO","sideQuest":true,"title":"Desktop Outpost","tagline":"Side quest, open once Cart Caverns is behind you: what to do when the thing you must test is not a web page.","teaches":"pywinauto, the Windows accessibility tree, and how the same layering applies outside the browser","source":["guide §12 Testing desktop apps","desktop/pages","desktop/helpers","desktop/run_tests.py"],"requires":["cart-caverns"],"map":{"x":90,"y":12},"objectives":["Say why Playwright cannot drive a native window, and what pywinauto does instead","Find a control by its accessibility name, the desktop cousin of get_by_role()","Apply the same layering off the web: tests assert, page objects own every control","Name what you give up outside the browser - no auto-waiting, no headless, no CI on a locked desktop","Decide when a desktop test is worth its cost, and when a unit test would do the job better"],"plannedChallenges":["Why Playwright stops at the window frame","The accessibility tree, and how to look at it","Locating a control by name","Auto-waiting is gone: what replaces it","The same layering, a different driver","Quiz: when is a desktop test the right tool?"],"lesson":"<div class=\"callout warn\"><strong>This zone cannot run here</strong><p>Every other zone on this map executes your Python against a live DOM. This one <strong>cannot</strong>, and never will. The whole point of desktop automation is driving windows that belong to the operating system, and a web page is not allowed anywhere near them - which is exactly the wall this zone is about.</p><p>So Desktop Outpost is a <strong>read-only lesson and quiz</strong>. To run the real thing you need Windows, an unlocked desktop, and this repo checked out:</p><pre class=\"lang-text\"><code>pip install -r requirements-desktop.txt\npython desktop/run_tests.py</code></pre></div><h2>Why Playwright stops at the window frame</h2><p>Playwright drives <strong>browser engines</strong>. It speaks to Chromium, Firefox and WebKit through their debugging protocols. A native application&#x27;s window is not a web page: there is no DOM, no CSS selector, no <code>page.goto()</code>. No amount of Playwright configuration reaches a desktop window, so the answer is not a clever locator - it is a different tool.</p><p>On Windows, native apps expose their controls through <strong>UI Automation</strong>, the same accessibility layer that lets a screen reader announce &quot;button, Login&quot;. That layer is what desktop automation tools read, and it is why they can inspect and click an app&#x27;s controls without the app cooperating in any way.</p><p><code>pywinauto</code> talks to UI Automation directly, in pure Python, with nothing extra to run. That is what the kit&#x27;s <code>desktop/</code> folder uses.</p><h2>The idea you already have</h2><p>You have spent this whole quest learning to find elements <strong>by what they are and what they say</strong> rather than by where they sit in the markup. <code>get_by_role(&quot;button&quot;, name=&quot;Login&quot;)</code> is a question asked of the accessibility tree.</p><p>Desktop automation asks the same question of a different tree:</p><pre class=\"lang-python\"><code># Web, with Playwright\npage.get_by_role(&quot;button&quot;, name=&quot;Equals&quot;).click()\n\n# Desktop, with pywinauto\nwindow.child_window(title=&quot;Equals&quot;, control_type=&quot;Button&quot;).click_input()</code></pre><p>Different library, same instinct. If role locators made sense to you, this will too.</p><h2>What you give up outside the browser</h2><p>Be clear-eyed about the trade, because it is a real one:</p><ul><li><strong>No auto-waiting.</strong> Playwright&#x27;s retry loop - the thing Assertion</li></ul><p>Ridge was built around - does not exist here. You wait for a control to appear explicitly, and the discipline of <em>never</em> reaching for a fixed sleep matters more, not less.</p><ul><li><strong>No headless.</strong> UI Automation drives actual windows, so the screen</li></ul><p>must be logged in and awake. On a locked machine or a disconnected remote-desktop session, windows never become visible and everything times out. CI needs a dedicated interactive machine.</p><ul><li><strong>Windows only.</strong> Which is why the kit keeps <code>pywinauto</code> in its own</li></ul><p><code>requirements-desktop.txt</code> - so the web tests still install and run on macOS and Linux.</p><h2>What stays exactly the same</h2><p>The layering. <code>desktop/</code> is deliberately built as a mirror of the web track: tests assert, helpers run journeys, page objects own every control. Same architecture, different driver.</p><p>That is the real lesson of this outpost. The tool changed and the design did not - which is a good sign that the design was about testing, not about Playwright.</p><div class=\"callout note\"><strong>The long version</strong><p>Section 12 of <code>docs/qa-automation-guide.html</code> is the full tutorial: backends, the inspector, waiting strategies, and the finished Calculator suite.</p></div>","challenges":[]},{"id":"endgame-ship-it","order":11,"badge":"★","abbr":"SI","endgame":true,"title":"Endgame: Ship It","tagline":"Stop practising. Scaffold a real suite for an app you actually care about.","teaches":"Turning everything learned here into a working project for your own application","source":["scaffold.py","template/","prompts/","README.md"],"requires":["runners-gate"],"map":{"x":22,"y":83},"objectives":["Scaffold a fresh project for your own app and get its smoke test green","Choose which parts of the framework you actually need, and leave the rest out","Write the first three tests for a site nobody has written tests for yet","Use the kit's AI prompts to draft page objects without letting them draft your judgement","Leave behind a suite a colleague can run on day one without asking you how"],"plannedChallenges":["Pick the app: what makes a good first target","Scaffold it: python scaffold.py --name my-suite --url ...","Your first green smoke test against your own site","Which components do you actually need? (--include)","Prompting for page objects without outsourcing the thinking","The three tests worth writing first","Boss: hand your suite to someone else and watch them run it"],"lesson":"","challenges":[]}],"about":"<h2>About this quest</h2><p>This is the third track of the <a href=\"https://github.com/jamessaludario/qa-starter-kit-python\">QA starter kit</a>, alongside the 26 commented practice tests in <code>learn/</code> and the project scaffolder. It is the browser successor to <code>python tour.py</code>.</p><p>Everything you write here is <strong>real Playwright</strong>. The import line is the one the kit&#x27;s own tests use:</p><pre class=\"lang-python\"><code>from playwright.sync_api import Page, expect</code></pre><p>...so a solution you write on this page can be pasted into <code>tour-tests/</code> and run against a real Chromium against the real <a href=\"https://automationexercise.com\">automationexercise.com</a> without changing a character.</p><h2>How your Python runs in a browser</h2><p>Playwright drives real browsers from outside them, so it cannot run <em>inside</em> one. Three pieces stand in for it:</p><ul><li><strong>AutomationVille</strong>, the shop in the right-hand panel. A static</li></ul><p>look-alike of automationexercise.com with the same ids, class names and headings, so your locators transfer.</p><ul><li><strong>Pyodide</strong>, a real CPython compiled to WebAssembly. Your code is not</li></ul><p>simulated, pattern-matched or faked - it is executed.</p><ul><li>**<code>playwright_lite</code>**, a Playwright-shaped shim that drives the shop&#x27;s</li></ul><p>DOM. It implements auto-waiting, strict mode and Playwright&#x27;s own error messages, because reading those messages is half the skill.</p><p>The full design, including what it deliberately does <em>not</em> do, is in <code>site/ARCHITECTURE.md</code>.</p><h2>How you are marked</h2><p>Not by comparing your code to ours. Three independent things are checked:</p><ol><li><strong>What your test did</strong> - every action it performed on the page.</li><li><strong>Where the shop ended up</strong> - its state when your test finished.</li><li><strong>How it reads</strong> - an AST review against the kit&#x27;s conventions</li></ol><p>(no <code>time.sleep()</code>, <code>expect()</code> where <code>assert</code> would be flaky, locators kept out of tests).</p><p>Find a better locator than ours and you pass. Paste ours with a sleep bolted on and you do not.</p><h2>Privacy</h2><p>There is no account, no server and no analytics. Progress lives in this browser&#x27;s local storage and never leaves the machine. Export it from the <strong>Progress</strong> page if you want to move it or hand it in.</p>"};
